<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsarevo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsarevo is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, in Burgas Province near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> mountains. As Vasiliko it was a centre of the Strandzha Commune during the Preobrazhenie Uprising of 1903, and it passed to Bulgaria after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>. It was renamed Tsarevo in 1934 and was called Michurin from 1950 to 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsarevo.jpg" alt="Tsarevo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SpaceControl · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsarevo_-_panoramio_-_SpaceControl_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tsarevo is a town and seaside resort on a cove 70 km south-east of Burgas, on the southern Bulgarian Black Sea coast, at the eastern foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> mountains and a few kilometres from Strandzha Nature Park. It is the seat of the Municipality of Tsarevo in Burgas Province. Until 1950 the settlement was known as Vasiliko, in Greek Βασιλικό, and from 1950 to 1991 it was named Michurin, after the Soviet botanist Ivan Vladimirovich Michurin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Underwater surveys have found amphoras of the 4th to 6th century and red-polished pottery made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, which points to prosperous trade in late antiquity. The southern peninsula has the remains of a medieval fortress. The 12th-century Arab geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Muhammad al-Idrisi</a> first mentions the town as Vasiliko. In the 15th and 16th centuries it was an Ottoman port, and in 1662 the traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> described a square fortress on a ridge above the sea, surrounded by vineyards. Its cove could hold the largest ships but was avoided by sailors because it gave little shelter from the powerful eastern winds.</p><p>In 1787 an Austrian ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> reported 200 houses and a lively port, and in the first half of the 19th century the town had a fleet of 42 ships, ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a> and a watermill, and vineyards producing up to 6,000 pails of wine a year. A Greek school there was also attended by many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. A fire in 1882 destroyed almost the whole town, and the inhabitants rebuilt it on the peninsula of the northern cove, called Limnos. Between 1878 and 1912 it was the centre of the Ahtabolu kaza in the Kırkkilise sanjak of the Edirne Vilayet.</p><p>The town was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1913 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>. Its Greek population moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a>. A new wharf was built from 1927 to 1937 with financial aid from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-iii-of-bulgaria">Tsar Boris III</a>, and the town was renamed Tsarevo, a literal translation of Vasiliko, meaning royal place, in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsarevo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the liberated villages the rebels set up a provisional administration, later called the Strandzha Commune, with <b>Vasiliko</b> as a centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/strandzha-commune">Strandzha Commune</a></li></ul></section>
